Verdict on AUDIT-6 generalization:
  PARTIAL CONFIRM. The M3>=80 discriminator does correctly partition images
  into 4 firing / 16 non-firing groups, with non-firing images showing
  near-zero bytes delta and modest SSIM2 deltas (good — the gate doesn't
  spuriously fire). However, the lift mechanism itself produces SSIM2 cost
  on M3>=80 PHOTOS (clic_22ea12 -1.54 mean SSIM2) that wasn't present in
  the AUDIT-6 calibration set (codec_wiki + 5 text screens). The 2 CLIC
  M3>=80 cells (clic_0c49a5 M3=95.91, clic_22ea12 M3=105.30) are
  conceptually photos, not screenshots, and the W44-105 buttloop seed-scale
  lift is designed for text-class quantization profiles. A secondary
  discriminator (e.g. fcbr or edge-density to gate OUT photos with high M3
  but no text) is the Phase 2 candidate.

DO NOT (binding for future agents):
  - DO NOT default-flip AUDIT-6 OFF for the CLIC2025 M3>=80 wedge without
    first measuring the codec_wiki PROTECT cells (the W44-105 SHIP cluster).
    The 4-image AUDIT-6 calibration includes those PROTECT cells; the lift
    saves -20.7% bytes there. A blanket disable would lose that.
  - DO NOT raise the M3 threshold above 80 without re-checking codec_wiki
    (M3=145.73) — too-high a threshold disables the SCREEN_TEXT win
    AUDIT-6 was designed for.
  - DO NOT cite "FMA precision" for any byte/SSIM2 movement (per W44-66
    user correction). All measurements use the same f32 backend on both
    sides; deltas are structural dispatch choices, not numeric noise.
  - DO NOT replace gui.png with a different SCREEN_GRAPHICS image without
    documenting why; the alpha-handling fix in score_jxl is generally
    useful (catches future RGBA corpus additions).
